WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.025
A partner bank sends a file of payments every night.

2
00:00:06.025 --> 00:00:11.012
The quickest way to load it is one wide table, with every detail on every row.

3
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.729
Ben, Jonas and Farid paid twice each.

4
00:00:13.729 --> 00:00:18.104
So every email and every name is stored twice, once per payment.

5
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:22.432
Farid's surname has two d's, but the file spelled it with one.

6
00:00:22.432 --> 00:00:26.697
He calls support about payment 18, and the agent fixes that row.

7
00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:29.332
Payment 17 still has the old spelling.

8
00:00:29.332 --> 00:00:34.261
Now one email has two names, and the table can't tell you which one is true.

9
00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:38.280
A fact stored on many rows has to change on every one of them.

10
00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.924
Miss one, and the table contradicts itself.

11
00:00:40.924 --> 00:00:42.892
That's called an update anomaly.

12
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:44.762
Ask what the columns mean.

13
00:00:44.762 --> 00:00:50.388
An email belongs to one customer, so rows with the same email should have the same name.

14
00:00:50.567 --> 00:00:55.129
That rule is a functional dependency: the email determines the name.

15
00:00:55.129 --> 00:00:59.759
The payment id determines every column, because it names one payment.

16
00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:01.168
It's the table's key.

17
00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:05.131
The trouble is a dependency whose left side is not the key.

18
00:01:05.131 --> 00:01:10.617
Email to name is a fact about the customer, and this table stores it on every payment.

19
00:01:10.867 --> 00:01:13.251
So give that fact a table of its own.

20
00:01:13.251 --> 00:01:19.051
Move the email and the name into customers, one row per email, and make the email its key.

21
00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:21.711
But Farid's email comes with two names.

22
00:01:21.711 --> 00:01:25.222
His second row has the same key, and the key refuses it.

23
00:01:25.222 --> 00:01:28.481
Someone has to decide, once, which spelling is true.

24
00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:31.003
Haddad, with two d's, goes in once.

25
00:01:31.003 --> 00:01:38.813
The payments table keeps only the payment's own facts: its id, its amount, and the email that points at the customer.

26
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:45.897
If Farid's name changes again, one update changes one row, and both of his payments read it through a join.

27
00:01:46.067 --> 00:01:47.696
Find what determines what.

28
00:01:47.696 --> 00:01:53.397
Give each fact one home, keyed by what determines it, and point at it from everywhere else.
